    </tbody></table></div><p class="text-gray-500 text-xs mt-2">Monthly prices shown with 19% VAT (EU). Hourly billing starts at €0.010/hr on the VPS 500 G12. All plans include flatrate traffic, snapshots, a backup system, and a remote console. Verify current pricing and location availability on netcup.com before ordering.</p></div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Hardware, Networking, and Locations</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The G12 generation is a clear upgrade over older Netcup tiers. Every x86 plan uses DDR5 ECC RAM and NVMe storage, with interface speeds up to 2.5 Gbit/s on the entry plan and higher tiers scaling further. KVM virtualization means full root access and standard Linux distributions, plus custom ISO support through the Server Control Panel.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Networking is one of Netcup's quiet strengths. Each VPS can hold a public IPv4 address plus a /64 IPv6 subnet at no additional cost, and an IPv6-only configuration actually reduces the monthly price. A Cloud vLAN option covers private networking between your servers. Traffic is flatrate, with a fair-use rule: if average traffic over the last 24 hours exceeds 2 TB on the entry plan, a temporary throttle to 200 Mbit/s applies until the average drops.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Location choice now spans Vienna (VIE), Nuremberg (NUE), Amsterdam (AMS), Manassas in the USA (MNZ), and Singapore (SIN), with a "No preference Europe" option that lets Netcup place the server in any EU location. Availability varies by plan and location, so check the product page during checkout.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Control Panel, Snapshots, and Backups</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup's Server Control Panel (SCP) handles deployment, reinstallation, snapshots, and DNS. Snapshots use Copy-On-Write, which keeps them fast and space-efficient for rollback testing. A separate backup system is included with the VPS line, and local block storage can be attached and expanded when the built-in NVMe allocation is not enough.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">This is an unmanaged product. You are responsible for OS updates, firewall configuration, application security, and restoring from snapshots or backups when needed. For buyers who want a managed layer, Netcup is not the right fit — the value is in raw hardware, not hands-on support.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Contract Terms and Payment</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup offers hourly billing (paid monthly in advance, settled hourly on cancellation) and monthly contracts, but several configs — especially the cheaper G12 deals — carry a 12-month minimum contract period. Read the plan card carefully before ordering.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Payment options include SEPA direct debit, bank transfer, PayPal, and credit card. New orders are covered by a 30-day satisfaction guarantee, and vServer tariffs advertise a guaranteed minimum availability of 99.6% on an annual average (root servers list 99.9%). Support is ticket-based with no phone line — reliable but not instant.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Who Should Choose Netcup?</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Choose Netcup when you are comfortable with root access and want the most hardware per euro in Europe: ECC RAM, NVMe, flatrate traffic, and snapshots at prices that undercut most US clouds. It is a strong fit for Docker hosts, application servers, databases, and self-hosted services that can tolerate a 12-month commitment.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Skip Netcup if you need short-term flexibility, phone support, managed administration, or a provider with datacenters in Asia-Pacific beyond the single Singapore location. For short-lived projects, an hourly-billed cloud like RackNerd or Vultr is easier to walk away from.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Netcup vs RackNerd</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RackNerd</a> is the better baseline for buyers who want ultra-low annual specials, US datacenter coverage, and no minimum-contract friction. Netcup wins when hardware quality (DDR5 ECC), flatrate traffic, EU locations, and snapshot tooling matter more than the lowest possible invoice.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">For a European production workload with a 12-month runway, Netcup's VPS 1000 G12 at €10.37/mo delivers more RAM and NVMe per euro than most US-based budget hosts. For a throwaway test box or a month-to-month project, RackNerd remains the cleaner choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-gray-800 pt-8 mb-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Our Verdict</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Netcup earns 4.4/5 for self-managed users in Europe. The G12 hardware stack — DDR5 ECC RAM, NVMe, flatrate traffic, snapshots, a backup system, and five locations — is exceptional value for the price. The 12-month minimum contract on many configs, fair-use throttle rule, and ticket-only support keep it below the top tier.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Before ordering, confirm the minimum contract period for the exact plan and location you want, and set up independent backups for anything irreplaceable. If those checks fit your operating model, Netcup is one of the best budget KVM values in Europe in 2026.</p>
    </div>

